+## Cross-cluster composition
+
+- Hook chain: the bounded read in cli is defeated by the shell command in bootstrap. Cluster 2 judged `read_stdin_payload` correct: a 64 KiB cap, a 2 s timeout, after printing. Cluster 1 judged `hook_command` correct against §2.3. Composed, they are not. The installed Claude command starts with `p=$(cat)`, an unbounded shell read that runs before the binary prints anything. So the bounded read never protects the production chain, and a held-open stdin blocks `/seat` until Claude's 10 s hook timeout kills the whole hook. That contradicts Unchanged behavior ("never fails or blocks its session") and the hg-blw.12 held-open acceptance. The only held-open test (tests/inspect_loaded.rs `held_open_stdin_still_prints_and_exits`) runs the bare binary, not the installed command. Claude Code closes stdin after writing the payload, so the practical reach is low. But the guarantee is claimed and tested on a path production never takes. This is cluster 11's Must fix, carried with the code-side evidence.
+- Codex identity seam: observe (stem of the Herdr rollout path) and the hook (payload `session_id`) produce different forms. loaded.rs compares `stem(loaded) == stem(occupant)`, which cannot detect a prefixed rollout stem. The design asked hg-blw.12 to record which form a real or documented payload uses, and no such record exists at head. When Herdr reports a bare id, or the id comes from the `resume <id>` argv, the forms agree and Codex reaches `current`/`stale`.
+- roster.live threads state: `LazyMap::inspect` (compose.rs ~1104-1120) updates `seat_view_error` only on Ok, Rejected and Unsupported. A `map()` failure (Disconnected, discovery) returns through `?` inside the async block and leaves the slot unchanged. The handler (roster_live.rs:64-82) reads only that slot. With threads down it therefore reports `live_unavailable: null` and empty or stale refusals. I re-verified this at head.
+- Integrity exit under `--live`: the daemon-free roster maps an integrity `StoreError` during scope resolution to exit 6 (`inspect::fail`). The daemon handler maps every `Scope::resolve` error to `bad_request`, which is exit 1. Both use the same Scope resolver, so the two modes disagree. Reach is low, and cluster 3 deferred it.
+- `ops` upgrade skew: cli `ops` now requires `head` in the `ops.list` reply, which only the new plan handler sends, and IPC_VERSION is unchanged. Plain `ops` therefore errors against a daemon still running the old binary until it restarts. The failure is safe. Cluster 2 deferred it.
+- `instructions stack --clone <name>`: cli resolves the clone name across the whole instance, while inspect `stack::build` requires that clone to belong to the seat. A same-named clone in another seat makes the name form ambiguous. Cluster 2 deferred it.
+- Doctor and setup agree on what "current" means. Both key on the `--stdin-payload` command shape through `owned_hook_current`, so `setup` rewrites exactly what `doctor` flags as outdated.
+- Envelope and live roster: the CLI deserializes the daemon's `roster` into the same `Roster` type and wraps it in the shared envelope. The daemon never builds a second roster. That matches the Roster key abstraction.
+- Open beads: hg-blw.10 (README and CLI help) and hg-blw.11 (integration sweep) are still open at head. README has no change in the diff. No baseline row depends on them alone, because the commands carry clap help. Cluster 11 notes that hg-blw.10 copies the design text, so the design drift it lists would spread into the README if hg-blw.10 lands first.
